Building Vacuum-IM
==================

CMake is the supported build system. Run the commands below from the source
root. GitHub Actions is the supported CI service.

Requirements
------------
  * CMake 3.21 or newer, a C++17 compiler, and Ninja for the supplied presets.
  * Qt 6.8 or newer: Widgets, Network, Xml, Concurrent, LinguistTools, PrintSupport,
    Multimedia and Sql. Svg is optional.
  * Linux: X11 and XScreenSaver headers; Qt private headers are not required.
  * System zlib, minizip and libidn are used when found; otherwise bundled
    copies are built (see src/thirdparty/README.md for versions and provenance).
    QtLockedFile and QxtGlobalShortcut are always built from
    the bundled sources against the application's Qt version.
  * Hunspell is the preset spellchecker backend. A bundled copy is available
    if the system library is absent. System Hunspell must be version 1.6 or newer.
  * GStreamer 1.20+ with SDP and WebRTC development files for voice calls.
    Release presets require voice calls; custom/development builds may use
    PLUGIN_voicecalls=AUTO or OFF. Runtime plugins include webrtc, dtls,
    srtp, nice, opus and rtp.
  * Adium message styles are currently disabled: they require Qt WebKit,
    which is not available in Qt 6.

Linux
-----
Example build dependencies on Ubuntu 26.04:

  sudo apt install build-essential cmake ninja-build pkg-config \
    qt6-base-dev qt6-svg-dev qt6-multimedia-dev \
    qt6-tools-dev qt6-tools-dev-tools libgl-dev libx11-dev libxss-dev \
    zlib1g-dev libminizip-dev libidn-dev libhunspell-dev \
    libgstreamer1.0-dev libgstreamer-plugins-base1.0-dev \
    libgstreamer-plugins-bad1.0-dev gstreamer1.0-plugins-base \
    gstreamer1.0-plugins-good gstreamer1.0-plugins-bad gstreamer1.0-nice

The same package list works on Debian 13 (trixie), which provides Qt 6.8.

Configure and build:

  cmake --preset release
  cmake --build --preset release --parallel

Install into a local staging directory:

  cmake --install build/release --prefix "$PWD/stage"
  ./stage/bin/vacuum

The installation contains bin/, lib/ and share/. Preserve this layout when
moving it. Linux builds use system Qt and other shared system libraries;
copying the installation tree does not bundle those dependencies.

A binary release needs a documented architecture, Linux/glibc baseline and
Qt build version. Qt's public API generally supports running binaries built
with an older Qt 6 on a newer Qt 6, not the reverse. The X11 code uses
QNativeInterface::QX11Application, which does not require private headers but
is excluded from Qt's source/binary compatibility guarantees for native
interfaces. Rebuilding from source uses the locally installed Qt version.

Windows
-------
Use a developer terminal for the compiler matching the installed Qt 6 kit
(for example, the x64 Visual Studio tools terminal for an MSVC x64 Qt kit).
Ensure Ninja is on PATH and point CMake at that Qt kit:

  cmake --preset release -DCMAKE_PREFIX_PATH=C:/Qt/6.x.y/msvc2022_64 \
    -DCMAKE_INSTALL_PREFIX=C:/vacuum-stage
  cmake --build --preset release --parallel
  cmake --install build/release

The executable is installed as C:/vacuum-stage/vacuum/vacuum.exe. The same
configure/build/install commands can be used with a matching MinGW Qt kit.

Windows archive builds require matching
GStreamer MSVC runtime/development files. Pass their common prefix when
configuring (the CI job installs Qt 6.12.0 and GStreamer 1.28.8):

  cmake --preset release -DCMAKE_PREFIX_PATH=C:/Qt/6.12.0/msvc2022_64 \
    -DVACUUM_GSTREAMER_ROOT=C:/gstreamer/1.0/msvc_x86_64

The Qt installation action pins an upstream aqtinstall revision because its
released 3.3.0 version cannot read the repository layout used by Qt 6.11+.

The bundled GStreamer plugins and scanner are selected automatically by the
voice-call plugin. End users do not need an installed Qt/GStreamer SDK or
manual PATH changes. Set BUNDLE_WINDOWS_DEPENDENCIES=OFF to disable deployment.
The Windows installer pin is 1.28.8, the latest published MSVC binary; Linux
uses the newer 1.28.9 system packages.

Release archives
----------------
Build and package using the release preset:

  cmake --preset release
  cmake --build --preset release --parallel
  cpack --config build/release/CPackConfig.cmake -C Release

Archives appear in build/release/packages/: .tar.gz on Linux, .zip on Windows.
Names include the application version/suffix, source commit, OS and architecture.
CPack stages the installation itself; no administrator installation is needed.
The application SDK and optional maintainer tools are excluded by the preset.

Both formats include build-info.json listing the expected application plugins.
Linux also includes RUNTIME-REQUIREMENTS.txt with Qt, glibc, GStreamer and
resolved shared-library versions. Linux archives contain Vacuum's own library,
plugins and resources, while Qt and GStreamer remain system dependencies.

The Linux CI job uses a pinned Arch Linux image and the 2026/11/14 repository
snapshot, matching the current development system (Qt 6.12, glibc 2.44).
This initial archive targets that system-library baseline. Runtime packages on
Arch include:

  sudo pacman -S qt6-base qt6-svg qt6-multimedia qt6-multimedia-ffmpeg \
    qt6-wayland hunspell libidn minizip gst-plugins-base gst-plugins-good \
    gst-plugins-bad libnice

Extract the complete archive and run bin/vacuum on Linux, or vacuum/vacuum.exe
on Windows. The Windows archive includes Qt platform/media/TLS/SQLite plugins,
compiler DLLs and the GStreamer dependencies needed for voice calls.

Distribution packages
---------------------
Native packages use the system Qt, GStreamer and other libraries, and install
under /usr. Packaging files live in src/packages/linux/arch (PKGBUILD) and
src/packages/linux/debian. They can be built locally in the CI containers:

  .github/scripts/build-arch-package.sh   # in .github/containers/linux.Dockerfile
  .github/scripts/build-deb.sh            # in .github/containers/debian.Dockerfile

The scripts package the committed HEAD (git archive), so commit first. The
package version comes from src/definitions/version.h. Builds have a snapshot
suffix with the commit date and hash unless VACUUM_RELEASE=1 is set. Outputs
are written to build/arch/ and build/deb/.

Continuous integration
----------------------
The CI workflow runs on every push and pull request. It builds only the Arch
Linux archive, checks the extracted archive, and uploads it as a workflow
artifact.

The Packages workflow builds the Arch package, the Debian 13 package and the
Windows archive. It runs when a v* tag is pushed, or manually from the Actions
tab. Each Linux package is installed and started headless; the Debian package
is installed on a clean Debian image, which checks its declared dependencies.
A tag also creates a draft GitHub release with all packages attached; tags
containing "-" (for example v1.3.0-alpha) are marked as pre-releases. The Arch
build must succeed for a release. Debian or Windows failures are listed in
the draft's notes instead of blocking it. Manual runs keep the packages as
workflow artifacts only.

The Windows check temporarily hides the Qt/GStreamer SDKs and removes
development PATH entries. The archive check script can also be run locally:

  python .github/scripts/check_archive.py /path/to/archive.tar.gz

CI caches the Arch and Debian containers' dependency layers, the Windows Qt installation,
and the installed GStreamer SDK. Changing the Dockerfile or GStreamer installer
script invalidates the corresponding preparation cache; Qt's cache key includes
its version, architecture and selected modules. The GStreamer SDK is saved as
soon as installation succeeds, even if a later compilation step fails.
Windows configuration uses pkg-config.exe from that SDK.

Compilation uses ccache for the CI archive build (a cache capped at 1 GiB)
and sccache with GitHub's cache backend on Windows. Linux package builds
compile from scratch. Compiler caches reuse object files based on
compiler, options, source and headers. CMake configuration, linking, packaging
and archive validation still run. Build directories are not cached. The first
run populates the caches; later runs report hits/misses in their compiler-cache
statistics. Cache entries can be removed from the repository's Actions cache UI.


Development and configuration
-----------------------------
To run directly from the build directory:

  cmake --preset dev
  cmake --build --preset dev --parallel
  ./build/dev/vacuum

Presets are conveniences; other CMake generators remain supported:

  cmake -S . -B build/custom -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release
  cmake --build build/custom --config Release --parallel
  cmake --install build/custom --config Release --prefix /path/to/stage

Useful configure options (pass as -DNAME=value):

The former FORCE_BUNDLED_MINIZIP option is replaced by selecting the BUNDLED
dependency provider.

  VACUUM_DEPENDENCY_PROVIDER=AUTO
                                Prefer system zlib, MiniZip, libidn and Hunspell;
                                build bundled copies when development files
                                are absent (default).
  VACUUM_DEPENDENCY_PROVIDER=SYSTEM
                                Require system development packages for those
                                libraries; used by Linux release CI.
  VACUUM_DEPENDENCY_PROVIDER=BUNDLED
                                Build those libraries from the source tree;
                                used by Windows release CI.
  INSTALL_SDK=ON                Install plugin development headers/helpers
                                (OFF in the supplied presets).
  INSTALL_DOCS=OFF              Skip installed documentation.
  INSTALL_ARCHIVE_INFO=OFF      Skip build-info.json and RUNTIME-REQUIREMENTS.txt
                                (used by distribution packages).
  INSTALL_LIB_DIR=lib64         Override the Unix library directory.
  PLUGIN_<name>=OFF             Disable a plugin.
  PLUGIN_voicecalls=ON          Require voice-call dependencies (release default).
  PLUGIN_voicecalls=AUTO        Build voice calls if dependencies are found.
  SPELLCHECKER_BACKEND=HUNSPELL Select hunspell, enchant, aspell or macspell.
  LANGS="uk;de"                 Select translations (default: all languages).
  RUN_FROM_BUILD_DIR=ON         Use source resources and build-tree plugins.
  BUILD_TRANSLATION_TOOLS=ON    Build optional maintainer utilities.

For multi-configuration generators, always pass --config Release (or Debug)
when building and installing. Keep personal settings in CMakeUserPresets.json.

Translations and plugin maintenance
-----------------------------------
Normal builds compile enabled translations into .qm files and installation
copies them into the application tree. To compile only translations:

  cmake --build build/release --target translations

To update existing .ts files from the source (this modifies source files):

  cmake --build build/release --target updatets

Both targets honor LANGS and LANG_<language>. Per-component targets such as
updatets_vacuum and translations_vacuum are also available. Translation
synchronization scripts remain separate from the build.

Optional maintainer tools can be built with:

  cmake --preset release -DBUILD_TRANSLATION_TOOLS=ON
  cmake --build build/release --target autotranslate txprepare

The executables are in build/release/src/tools/ and are not installed with
the application. Run either without arguments for its usage.

To add a plugin, follow an existing plugin's CMakeLists.txt and source-list
.cmake file, then register its directory in src/plugins/plugin_list.cmake.
